--TEST--
Core\SignedCookie::seal then ::open is the identity over a sweep of payloads — the empty one, one carrying the cookie delimiters, one whose octets outnumber its characters and one long enough to cross a kilobyte — and two different cookies of one value both open to that value — `rule:security/protocol-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Invariance over a sweep. The round trip is asserted against *the payload
// itself* on every line of this case and never against a frozen cookie: the
// nonce is fresh per call and the ring is generated per run, so there is no
// literal a cookie could be compared to. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/signed_cookie.rs`
// owns why that is the shape — the answer is the plaintext that went in, and
// the ciphertext around it is deliberately unstable.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];

// The axis here is the *shape* of the payload rather than which punctuation it
// carries: nothing, then the delimiters a `Set-Cookie` header parses on, then a
// payload whose octet count is nearly twice its character count, then one long
// enough that its cookie crosses a kilobyte. A member that trimmed, padded or
// re-encoded on the way through prints exactly the right thing for a short
// ASCII session id and fails at one of the other three.
array<string> $values = [
    "",
    "id=42; theme=dark; last.seen=2026-09-01",
    "пароль=σ; 日本.語",
    Core\Str::repeat("user=ada; role=admin; ", 64),
];

int $identical = 0;
int $reopened = 0;
foreach ($values as string $payload) {
    string $cookie = Core\SignedCookie::seal($payload, $ring);
    if (Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie, $ring) == $payload) {
        $identical = $identical + 1;
    }
    // Sealed a second time, and opened against the payload again rather than
    // against the first cookie. The two cookies differ — the case next door
    // pins that — so this is the assertion that says which of the two the
    // program is entitled to: the plaintext is the invariant and the cookie is
    // not. A member that answered a cached plaintext keyed by the cookie text
    // would pass the first count and fail here.
    string $again = Core\SignedCookie::seal($payload, $ring);
    if (Core\SignedCookie::open($again, $ring) == $payload) {
        $reopened = $reopened + 1;
    }
}
echo "intact ", $identical, " of ", Core\Arr::count($values), "\n";
echo "a second cookie of the same value opens to it too: ", $reopened, " of ", Core\Arr::count($values), "\n";

// And this is what keeps the two counts from being four ASCII round trips in a
// row. The third payload's octets outnumber its characters by nearly two to
// one, so a member that sealed `Core\Str::length` octets — or that measured the
// buffer it decrypted in characters — truncates it, and the fourth is past
// every 3-octet base64 group boundary and past the 40 octets of AEAD overhead
// several times over. Printed rather than asserted, because the numbers are
// the reason the sweep is not vacuous.
echo "the multibyte payload is ", Core\Str::length($values[2]), " characters and ",
     Core\Bytes::length(Core\Encoding::encodeText($values[2], Core\Charset::Utf8)), " octets\n";
echo "the long payload is ", Core\Str::length($values[3]), " characters, its cookie ",
     Core\Str::length(Core\SignedCookie::seal($values[3], $ring)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
intact 4 of 4
a second cookie of the same value opens to it too: 4 of 4
the multibyte payload is 14 characters and 27 octets
the long payload is 1408 characters, its cookie 1931
